{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli a crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born around 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. He was under the regency of his mother until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III was a member of the House of Toulouse and was related to several other prominent nobles in the Holy Land. He was a first cousin of King Amalric I of Jerusalem and was also related to the Princes of Antioch. *Reign* As Count of Tripoli Raymond III was known for his military prowess and his ability to maintain the independence of his county. He was involved in several conflicts with neighboring states including the Principality of Antioch and the Zengid dynasty.\n\nIn 1174 Raymond III was appointed as regent for King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem who was suffering from leprosy. *Regency for Baldwin IV* Raymond III played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the regency and was a key advisor to the young king. He was a member of the faction that opposed the influence of the Latin Patriarch of Jerusalem and the Knights Templar. Raymond III's regency was marked by a series of conflicts with the Zengid dynasty and the ruler of Egypt Saladin.\n\nRaymond III was also known for his involvement in the Battle of Hattin in 1187 where he played a key role in the defeat of the Kingdom of Jerusalem at the hands of Saladin. After the battle Raymond III escaped to Tyre where he died in 1187. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III Prince of Antioch. Raymond III's legacy was marked by his military prowess and his ability to maintain the independence of the County of Tripoli."